                        "cot": "\n\nB) Gravity causes gases and dust particles to condense into spheres.\n\nReasoning:\n\nStars are formed from clouds of gas and dust in space. These clouds are called nebulae. Gravity plays a crucial role in the formation of stars. As the gas and dust particles in the nebulae come closer to each other, the force of gravity between them increases. This causes the particles to attract each other and come together. As more and more particles come together, the mass of the cloud increases, and so does the force of gravity. This process continues until the cloud becomes dense enough for the particles to start sticking together and forming clumps. These clumps eventually become the cores of new stars.\n\nTherefore, the statement that best describes the role of gravity in the formation of stars is B) Gravity causes gases and dust particles to condense into spheres.",
