                        "cot": "\n\nThe answer is B) the Moon.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- The phenomenon described in the question is known as tides, which are the periodic rise and fall of sea levels caused by the gravitational pull of celestial bodies on Earth's oceans.\n- The two main celestial bodies that affect tides are the Sun and the Moon, but the Moon has a stronger influence because it is closer to Earth and has a stronger gravitational pull.\n- When the Moon is directly overhead or on the opposite side of Earth (at full or new moon), its gravitational pull on the oceans is strongest, causing high tides (known as spring tides).\n- When the Moon is at a right angle to the Earth-Sun line (at first or third quarter moon), its gravitational pull on the oceans is weaker, causing low tides (known as neap tides).\n- Therefore, the differences in water level that Miriam observes at the beach are primarily due to the gravitational influence of the Moon.",
