                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe destruction of a forest habitat involves cutting down trees and other vegetation. Trees and other plants absorb carbon dioxide (CO_{2}) from the atmosphere during photosynthesis and release oxygen (O_{2}) as a byproduct. Therefore, significant destruction of a forest habitat will result in a decrease in the amount of CO_{2} absorbed from the atmosphere and a decrease in the amount of O_{2} released into the atmosphere. \n\nAs a result, the answer to the question is A) CO_{2}. When trees and other vegetation are destroyed, the amount of CO_{2} in the atmosphere will increase because there are fewer plants to absorb it. This increase in CO_{2} can contribute to global warming and climate change. \n\nNO_{2} and N_{2} are not directly related to forest destruction. NO_{2} is a harmful air pollutant that is primarily produced by human activities such as transportation and industrial processes. N_{2} is a naturally occurring gas that makes up about 78% of the Earth's atmosphere and is not affected by forest destruction. \n\nO_{2} is released by plants during photosynthesis, but it is not a greenhouse gas and is not directly related to climate change. While the destruction of a forest habitat can lead to a decrease in the amount of O_{2} released into the atmosphere, this is not the main concern when it comes to the impact of deforestation on the environment.",
                        "answers": [
