                    {
                        "id": "409369b0-b5c3-4fc6-b899-9b6f0c04ba9d",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " \n\nFirst, what is the evidence of extensive glaciation? \n\nThere are a few different types of evidence that scientists have used to suggest that there was extensive glaciation at the end of the Permian period. One type of evidence is the presence of glacial deposits (rocks and sediments that were deposited by glaciers) in areas that were once thought to be too warm for glaciers to form. Another type of evidence is the presence of \"dropstones\" in marine sediments - these are large rocks that fell from the melting glaciers into the ocean and were then deposited on the ocean floor.\n\nBased on this evidence, scientists believe that there was a period of extensive glaciation at the end of the Permian period.\n\nNow, let's think about how this could have disrupted ocean ecosystems. \n\nOne way that glaciation could have disrupted ocean ecosystems is by causing a steep drop in ocean salinity and density. This could happen if a lot of fresh water from melting glaciers entered the ocean, causing the ocean water to become less salty and less dense. This could have a major impact on ocean life, as many marine organisms rely on a certain level of salinity and density in the water to survive.\n\nAnother way that glaciation could have disrupted ocean ecosystems is by causing a steep drop in ocean volume and area. This could happen if the glaciers melted and caused the sea level to rise, flooding coastal areas and reducing the overall area of the ocean. This could also have a major impact on ocean life, as many marine organisms rely on a certain amount of space to live and move around.\n\nFinally, glaciation could have increased exposure of oceans to ultraviolet radiation. This could happen if the glaciers melted and caused the sea level to rise, flooding coastal areas and exposing more of the ocean to the sun. This could also have a major impact on ocean life, as many marine organisms are sensitive to ultraviolet radiation and could be killed by exposure to too much of it.\n\nSo, in conclusion, the evidence of extensive glaciation at the end of the Permian period could have disrupted ocean ecosystems in a number of ways.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "b6684d35-f85c-41d7-b5ef-97eb38b11c2a",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " A, B, and C.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/02/27 14:48:39",
                        "api_service": "openai",
                        "model": "{'name': 'text-davinci-002', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
