                        "cot": "\n\nGlaciation involves the formation of large ice sheets on land, which can cause a drop in sea level due to the water being locked up in ice. This would suggest that option B, a steep drop in ocean volume and area, is a possible effect of glaciation. However, this alone may not be enough to explain extinctions at the end of the Permian period.\n\nAnother possible effect of glaciation is a steep drop in ocean salinity and density. As ice sheets form on land, they remove freshwater from the ocean, which can cause the remaining seawater to become more saline and denser. This can disrupt ocean ecosystems by affecting the circulation of water and nutrients, which can in turn impact the survival of marine organisms.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A) steep drop in ocean salinity and density.",
