                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand the properties of sound waves. \n\n- Frequency: the number of cycles (vibrations) per second, measured in Hertz (Hz)\n- Amplitude: the maximum displacement of a wave from its equilibrium position, measured in decibels (dB)\n- Wavelength: the distance between two consecutive points on a wave that are in phase (e.g. two peaks or two troughs), measured in meters (m)\n- Speed in air: the speed at which sound waves travel through air, which is approximately 343 meters per second at room temperature\n\nNow, let's consider the scenario described in the question. \n\n- The two sound waves have the same pitch, which means they have the same frequency. Therefore, we can eliminate option A. \n- The second sound wave is louder, which means it has a greater amplitude. Therefore, option B is the correct answer. \n- The wavelength of a sound wave depends on its frequency and the speed of sound in the medium it is traveling through. Since the frequency is the same for both waves and the speed of sound in air is constant, we can eliminate option C. \n- Similarly, the speed of sound in air is constant, so we can eliminate option D. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Amplitude.",
