                        "cot": "\n\nThe dominant trait is black fur, which means that if an individual has at least one copy of the black fur allele (B), it will express black fur. The recessive trait is white fur, which means that an individual must have two copies of the white fur allele (bb) to express white fur.\n\nThe two black guinea pigs (Bb) each have one copy of the black fur allele and one copy of the white fur allele. When they are crossed, their offspring will inherit one allele from each parent, randomly. \n\nThere are four possible combinations of alleles that the offspring can inherit: BB, Bb, bB, and bb. \n\n- BB: This offspring will inherit a black fur allele from each parent and will express black fur. \n- Bb: This offspring will inherit a black fur allele from one parent and a white fur allele from the other parent. Since black fur is dominant, this offspring will still express black fur. \n- bB: This offspring will inherit a white fur allele from one parent and a black fur allele from the other parent. Since black fur is dominant, this offspring will still express black fur. \n- bb: This offspring will inherit a white fur allele from each parent and will express white fur. \n\nTherefore, out of the four possible combinations of alleles, three of them will result in black fur (BB, Bb, and bB) and only one will result in white fur (bb). \n\nSo, the fur color of the offspring will be 75% black (BB, Bb, and bB) and 25% white (bb). \n\nThe correct answer is D) 75% black, 25% white.",
