                    {
                        "id": "7b26e868-c88f-48ac-bcb0-8fdc2072a140",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nWhen the heat energy of a gas is decreased, its particles will slow down and move closer together. This can lead to a decrease in the gas's volume and pressure. \n\nA) Condensation occurs when a gas is cooled and its particles lose energy, causing them to come together and form a liquid. So, decreasing the heat energy of a gas could lead to condensation. \n\nB) Evaporation occurs when a liquid is heated and its particles gain energy, causing them to escape into the air as a gas. This is the opposite of what happens when the heat energy of a gas is decreased, so evaporation is not a likely result. \n\nC) Radiation is the transfer of energy through electromagnetic waves, and is not directly related to the heat energy of a gas. So, decreasing the heat energy of a gas would not necessarily cause radiation. \n\nD) Vaporization is the process of a liquid turning into a gas, which is also the opposite of what happens when the heat energy of a gas is decreased. So, vaporization is not a likely result. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) condensation.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "99411642-5778-4d4f-85b1-7e5bfc79b932",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) condensation.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/11 02:43:40",
