                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the purpose of observing a soil sample.\nObserving a soil sample is usually done to examine its physical and chemical properties, such as texture, color, structure, and the presence of organic matter, minerals, and microorganisms.\n\nStep 2: Consider the characteristics of the tools listed.\nA) Camera: Takes pictures or videos of the soil sample, but may not provide enough detail or magnification to see small features.\nB) Compass: Measures direction and orientation, but is not relevant for observing soil properties.\nC) Hand lens: A small magnifying glass that can provide a closer look at the soil sample, revealing its texture, structure, and other features.\nD) Telescope: A device used for viewing distant objects, not suitable for observing soil samples.\n\nStep 3: Evaluate the suitability of each tool for observing a soil sample.\nA) Camera: Not the best tool for observing soil samples as it may not provide enough detail or magnification.\nB) Compass: Not relevant for observing soil properties.\nC) Hand lens: The best tool for observing soil samples as it can provide a closer look at the soil sample, revealing its texture, structure, and other features.\nD) Telescope: Not suitable for observing soil samples.\n\nStep 4: Choose the best tool for observing a soil sample.\nBased on the above analysis, the best tool for observing a soil sample is the hand lens (option C).",
                        "answers": [
